**Chapter 9: Patent Claims Defining the Monolithic DLD-Impedance Cytometry Platform**

1. A monolithic microfluidic chip for label-free isolation and biophysical characterization of circulating tumor cells (CTCs) from whole blood, comprising:  
(a) a substrate stack including a low-noise dielectric layer selected from fused silica or thermally oxidized silicon, anodically bonded to a fluidic capping layer;  
(b) a deterministic lateral displacement (DLD) array integrated within a microchannel of said chip, said array comprising staggered micropillars arranged at a critical angle θc of 4° to 8°, each pillar having a diameter of 8–12 µm and defining inter-pillar gaps of 5–7 µm, configured to displace CTCs ≥12 µm at flow rates of 10–50 µL/min while allowing leukocytes <10 µm to flow undeflected, thereby achieving CTC recovery >90% and leukocyte contamination <5%;  
(c) coplanar gold microelectrodes embedded flush within the channel floor in a post-DLD detection zone located ≤500 µm downstream of the last pillar row, each electrode having a width of 10–20 µm and spacing of 15–25 µm, configured for multi-frequency impedance cytometry across 0.1–10 MHz with signal-to-noise ratio >20 dB;  
(d) a zwitterionic sulfobetaine-based surface passivation layer covalently grafted to all fluid-contacting surfaces, reducing non-specific protein adsorption to <5 ng/cm² and preserving cell viability >95%; and  
(e) electrical feedthroughs comprising metal traces routed to edge pads and aligned with external impedance analyzer ports via anisotropic conductive film interposers, enabling portable, battery-powered operation.

2. The chip of claim 1, further comprising a portable microcontroller-driven signal acquisition module operably connected to said electrodes, configured to apply swept-sine AC excitation (10–100 mVrms) synchronized with cell transit detection and to compute membrane capacitance and cytoplasmic conductivity via Cole-Cole modeling for real-time CTC classification.

3. The chip of claim 1, wherein said DLD array is adapted for environmental pathogen detection by resizing inter-pillar gaps to 1–3 µm and recalibrating impedance thresholds to distinguish microbial targets based on dielectric signatures, achieving detection limits of ≤10 CFU/mL in aqueous samples.

4. The chip of claim 1, further comprising a dual-inlet sheath-flow configuration with a secondary buffer inlet upstream of the DLD outlet, enabling on-chip buffer exchange during CTC elution to maintain physiological conditions and compatibility with downstream molecular assays.
